Generated by: Andrew Teylu <andrew@tey.lu>
Generated on: 2026-08-24
Generator: Model-driven synthetic Latendresse profile generator, with empirical structure extracted from the bundled canonical biological model.
Application: Synthetic linear-real and floating-point benchmarks modeled on the structure of the existing SMT-LIB QF_LRA Latendresse benchmarks [1], which originate in flux-balance-analysis and pathway-database model-completion applications [2].
Biological inspiration: Shewanella flux balance analysis with ungated objective, nutrient, and secretion selectors, specifically the shewanella-compact2 model in the existing SMT-LIB Latendresse family.
Target solver: cvc5
Time limit: 300

The model has 100 boxed real-valued reaction variables, 5 bounded real-valued 0/1 selectors (3 objective, 1 nutrient, and 1 secretion), and 75 exact mass-balance rows spanning 1 to 54 reactions. Selectors do not gate reactions. Bounds, selector disjunctions, and mass-balance rows occur in one compact top-level conjunction. The signed selector-only objective preserves the canonical +1000, -1, and -2 ratio under one positive size-dependent normalization.

Objective regime: interior. The selector-only Shewanella objective uses an attainable interior equality, making the QF_LRA instance satisfiable.
Numeric profile: small-flux. The same structural construction uses flux upper bound 8, separating small representable flux magnitudes from the canonical 1000-scale instances.

This file is a structural mixed floating-point/linear-real translation using QF_FPLRA. Real variables are replaced by (_ FloatingPoint 5 11) variables and arithmetic over them uses roundNearestTiesToEven. Numeric constants remain exact Reals beneath SMT-LIB to_fp conversions. Linear comparisons become floating-point comparisons; equality uses IEEE fp.eq. No finite-value guards are added.

Floating point is not the reals; satisfiability is not assumed to survive this translation. The translated status is unknown.

[1] Timothy King, Clark Barrett, and Bruno Dutertre, Simplex with Sum of Infeasibilities for SMT, FMCAD 2013.
[2] Mario Latendresse, Markus Krummenacker, Miles Trupp, and Peter D. Karp, Construction and completion of flux balance models from pathway databases, Bioinformatics 28(3), 388-396, 2012.
